---
/**
 * /market — CanaryOne Market.
 *
 * RESTRUCTURED 2026-08-13, from the review copy that briefly lived at /market-v3. The order is
 * the argument: the measured finding leads, because it is the only thing on the page nobody
 * else can publish, and the commercial ask sits directly under it rather than only at the foot.
 * Then market breadth, price against speed, capability, and the price history last, as the
 * closing evidence that this is a running system rather than as the opening hook.
 *
 * The model reference table went with that change. It was the "look up your own model" block —
 * model, companies, cheapest, dearest, spread, weights pulled, with brand marks — and it is in
 * git history if the lookup job turns out to be worth its screen. Deliberate, not an oversight.
 *
 * Section headings each carry one sentence a reader understands before inspecting the chart,
 * which is the rule that shaped this pass: charts support a statement, they do not hide one.
 *
 * Its ancestor's history follows, because the machinery is unchanged.
 *
 * Took over the /market route on 2026-08-11, replacing the tile-based version that had held it
 * since /prices was renamed. That page is in git history if any of it is wanted back; what
 * it could not do was show host-level history, which is where the disagreement between
 * companies actually lives, so one line per model was the average of a market and hid the
 * only interesting thing in it.
 *
 * STRUCTURE FOLLOWS THE OLD /prices PAGE, which was the reference for this design system:
 * one page-head, then ONE section, one column, and every block built from the existing
 * .tm-* Market primitives.
 *
 * Two mistakes from the first attempt, recorded so they are not repeated:
 *   .hub-card is display:flex/align-center/justify-center with a min-height — a LOGO TILE
 *   for the homepage hub row. Putting four paragraphs in it laid them out side by side and
 *   crushed each into a two-word column. It is not a content card.
 *   .boundary::before injects the literal text "your machine". It is the install box on the
 *   homepage, not a generic bordered container.
 *
 * Tabular content uses .tm-data-table. Every section is the shared shell in global.css —
 * .sec-copy, .claim, .sec-note, .panel and .evidence — which replaced this page's own
 * .tm-note prose and its fifteen .tm-coverage callouts on 2026-08-13.
 * Charts sit in .tm-panel, are build-time inline SVG, and ship no client JavaScript.
 *
 * Every figure is labelled metadata (free, read several times a day) or measured workload
 * (a paid run, dated). The two are never mixed in one sentence.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import market from '../data/market.json';
import { brandIconFor } from '../lib/model-brand';
import { selectorCss } from '../lib/selector';
import {
  spell, cap, times, moreBy, moreThan, mult, day, stamp, usd, usdCol, usd4, priceWords, int,
} from '../lib/prose';

/* Every formatter comes from one module. This page used to declare its own spell(), cap(),
   times(), mult(), usd4() and date formatter beside the copies in src/lib/prose.ts, which meant
   two implementations of the banding that decides how a ratio is rounded on a page with a
   number gate. See that file's header. */
const captured = stamp(market.captured_at);

const m = market.measured;
/* Only the scatter's fallback now. `market.first_token` is deliberately not read any more:
   it held the 182-fold first-token spread that the old pulse table led with, and both spec
   documents exclude latency as a headline because a thirty-minute rolling window read once
   presents as a property of the company. It stays in the payload for the internal pages. */
const lead = market.models[0];
const pair = m?.same_price_pair;

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Chart geometry. All five charts are build-time inline SVG on a viewBox, so the
   panel scales and no client JavaScript ships. Marks take their colour from
   global.css tokens only — DESIGN.md forbids a literal colour at a use site, and
   canary yellow is a fill behind dark text on cream rather than a data mark, so
   the series ink here is --text and --muted with --accent-deep kept for the one
   thing being singled out.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---- mark sizes ----
   Two radii for the whole page and no third. Every dot is either a data point or a data point
   being singled out, so five ad-hoc values (2.5, 4, 4.5, 5 and 6) were four too many and the
   charts read as separate exercises rather than one system.

   These are viewBox units, so they only render at a consistent size if every chart shares a
   scale factor. The wide charts are 800 units in a panel about 1072px across, which is 1.34.
   The tiles are a quarter of that width, so their viewBox is a quarter as wide — see TW —
   and the same R_SM draws the same number of screen pixels in both. */
const R_SM = 4, R_LG = 6;

/* 1. The hook: one small labelled chart per company, not one chart with 32 lines.
   The first version drew all 32 series in a single frame and was unreadable — no
   legend can name 32 lines, and this design system has no categorical palette to
   colour them with, because it is a two-colour system by design. Faceting is the
   fix: every company gets its own tile carrying its own name, so identity comes
   from a label instead of from a colour a reader has to look up. Every tile shares
   one y-scale, which is what keeps the heights comparable between them. */
const hook = market.hook;
const hkHosts = hook.hosts.filter((h: any) => h.last != null);
const hkN = hook.stamps.length;
/* One tile, drawn once per company. Small enough that 32 of them fit, tall enough that the
   shared vertical scale can actually separate a 50-cent company from a $2.31 one — at 52
   units the whole market compressed into about eleven pixels of height.

   190 units wide rather than 240, so a tile renders at roughly the same scale factor as the
   800-unit wide charts and R_SM draws the same size of dot in both. At 240 the endpoint dot
   and the line were visibly finer than every other mark on the page. */
const TW = 190, TH = 46, TP = 4;
const hkVals = hkHosts.flatMap((h: any) => h.points.filter((p: any) => p != null)) as number[];
const hkLo = Math.min(...hkVals), hkHi = Math.max(...hkVals);
/* Logarithmic, for the same reason the ladder is. Three companies ran a discount that took
   their price to a tenth of where it started, so the range across the week is twenty-nine
   fold and on a linear scale the other twenty-nine tiles come out as flat lines pinned to
   the top of their box. */
const lg = (v: number) => Math.log10(v);
const hkLoA = hkLo, hkHiA = hkHi * 1.04;
/* Spaced by clock rather than by read number. The reads are not evenly spread — seven landed
   on 10 August and one on 6 August — so equal steps stretched two days of discount across a
   third of the width. */
const hkT = hook.stamps.map((s: string) => new Date(s).getTime());
const hkT0 = hkT[0], hkT1 = hkT[hkT.length - 1];
const tx = (i: number) =>
  TP + (hkT1 === hkT0 ? 0 : (hkT[i] - hkT0) / (hkT1 - hkT0)) * (TW - TP * 2);
/* Shared across every tile. A per-tile scale would make a company sitting at 50 cents and
   one sitting at $2.31 draw the same picture, which is the opposite of the point. */
const ty = (v: number) =>
  TP + (1 - (lg(v) - lg(hkLoA)) / (lg(hkHiA) - lg(hkLoA))) * (TH - TP * 2);
/* A gap in a company's series breaks the path rather than interpolating across it. */
const hkPath = (points: (number | null)[]) => {
  let d = '', pen = false;
  points.forEach((v, i) => {
    if (v == null) { pen = false; return; }
    d += `${pen ? 'L' : 'M'}${tx(i).toFixed(1)} ${ty(v).toFixed(1)} `;
    pen = true;
  });
  return d.trim();
};
const byPrice = (a: any, b: any) => a.last - b.last;
const hkFlat = hkHosts.filter((h: any) => !h.moved).sort(byPrice);
const hkMoved = hkHosts.filter((h: any) => h.moved).sort(byPrice);
const hkLabel = (h: any) => h.provider + (h.variant ? ` ${h.variant}` : '');
/* The hover text for a dot on the flat-price strip. Name, price and two facts that are
   nowhere else on the page: what precision the company says it serves at, and how much
   context it gives you, which runs from 94K to 1024K across these same weights. */
const hkTip = (h: any) => [
  hkLabel(h),
  `${usd(h.last)} per 1M`,
  h.quant && h.quant !== 'unknown' ? `${h.quant} precision` : 'precision not stated',
  h.context ? `${Math.round(h.context / 1024)}K context` : null,
].filter(Boolean).join(' · ');
const hkTop = hkHosts.reduce((a: any, b: any) => (b.last > a.last ? b : a));
const hkBot = hkHosts.reduce((a: any, b: any) => (b.last < a.last ? b : a));
/* The deepest swing inside a single company's own week. Unnamed in the copy: the size of a
   move reproduces and which company made it does not. */
const hkSwing = Math.max(
  ...hkHosts.map((h: any) => {
    const v = h.points.filter((p: any) => p != null) as number[];
    return Math.max(...v) / Math.min(...v);
  }),
);
/* ---- the shared grid ----
   One label column and two value columns, used by three blocks below: the flat-price strip,
   the measured pair and the cohort strip. Declared here rather than inside the pair because
   all three read them, and a strip whose dots started at its own left edge would not line up
   under the comparison it belongs to. Hoisted after the flat strip tried to call fsx() at
   module scope and hit "Cannot access 'PC1' before initialization". */
const PAIR_W = 800, PAIR_H = 306;
const PL = 252;                        /* row labels end here */
const PC1 = 280, PC2 = 540, PCW = 220; /* two value columns, left edge and width */
/* Every value in a column shares that column's left edge, including the bar, so each column
   reads as one column. The equals sign sits in the gap between them. */
const PEQ = (PC1 + PC2) / 2;

/* 1a. The companies that never moved, as a stacked dot plot rather than 23 flat sparklines.
   A tile per company was a lot of space for "nothing happened": 23 boxes each drawing one
   horizontal line, where the only information was the line's height.

   Stacked rather than a single row, which is what the shape of the data forces. The 23 sit on
   just 11 distinct prices, nine of them at exactly $1.40, so one row would draw 11 visible
   dots and bury 12 companies underneath them where no hover could reach. Stacking makes the
   cluster the thing you see first, which is the finding this section was hiding.

   Vertical position therefore carries no meaning of its own; it is only how many companies
   share that price. Same grid as the cohort strip lower down: label column right-aligned to
   PL, data area between the hero's own column edges. */
const FS_DOT = R_SM, FS_GAP = 11, FS_PAD = 8;
const fsLo = hkFlat.length ? Math.min(...hkFlat.map((h: any) => h.last)) : 0;
const fsHi = hkFlat.length ? Math.max(...hkFlat.map((h: any) => h.last)) : 1;
/* Linear, not logarithmic. The whole range is three-fold, which a linear axis shows honestly
   and without a scale a reader has to be told about. Inset by FS_PAD so the cheapest dot does
   not sit half on top of the end of the axis rule. */
const fsx = (v: number) =>
  PC1 + FS_PAD
  + (fsHi === fsLo ? 0 : (v - fsLo) / (fsHi - fsLo)) * (PC2 + PCW - PC1 - FS_PAD * 2);

/* Row assignment, not grouping by equal price. Grouping only stacks dots whose prices match
   exactly, which left DeepInfra at $0.750, CoreWeave at $0.760 and AkashML at $0.770 drawn
   three pixels apart as one grey blob with three tooltips fighting over the same spot.
   Working left to right and dropping each dot into the lowest row where it clears the last
   dot already in that row handles equal and merely-close prices with one rule. Prices keep
   their true position on the axis; only the row is invented. */
const FS_MIN_DX = FS_DOT * 2 + 2;
const fsRowLastX: number[] = [];
const fsPlaced = [...hkFlat]
  .sort((a: any, b: any) => a.last - b.last || hkLabel(a).localeCompare(hkLabel(b)))
  .map((h: any) => {
    const x = fsx(h.last);
    let row = 0;
    while (fsRowLastX[row] !== undefined && x - fsRowLastX[row] < FS_MIN_DX) row++;
    fsRowLastX[row] = x;
    return { host: h, x, row };
  });
/* Sized from the tallest column so a night with a deeper cluster grows the panel instead of
   drawing dots off the top of it. */
const fsRows = fsPlaced.length ? 1 + Math.max(...fsPlaced.map((p) => p.row)) : 1;
const FS_AX = 20 + (fsRows - 1) * FS_GAP + 9;
const FS_H = FS_AX + 46;

/* Labels name the clusters only. Grouped on the exact listed price here rather than on the
   drawn rows, because "nine companies charge exactly this" is the claim; three companies
   whose prices merely round together is not. */
const fsStacks = Object.values(
  hkFlat.reduce((acc: Record<string, any>, h: any) => {
    const key = h.last.toFixed(3);
    (acc[key] ??= { price: h.last, hosts: [] }).hosts.push(h);
    return acc;
  }, {}),
).sort((a: any, b: any) => a.price - b.price);
const fsLabelled = fsStacks.filter((g: any) => g.hosts.length >= 3);
const fsBiggest = fsStacks.reduce((a: any, b: any) => (b.hosts.length > a.hosts.length ? b : a),
  { hosts: [] } as any);

/* 1b. The measured pair, as a hero comparison rather than a four-row table.
   Two columns held throughout, so the same two hosts occupy the same two positions in every
   row. The first two rows are the controls and carry an equals sign between them; the cost
   row is the reveal and is the only row drawn as bars, because length is what makes the gap
   land. Reasoning sits below a rule in small muted type: it is the mechanism, not the
   headline, and giving it equal weight was the old table's mistake.

   Both bars take one ink. Colouring the dearer one differently would encode the magnitude
   twice, once as length and once as hue, and burn the only free channel on something the
   bar already says. */
/* The sweep records a route tag like `z-ai/fp8`; the daily capture records the company's own
   display name for that same tag. The sweep model and the hook model are the same model, so
   the names are already on the page and there is no reason to show a reader the raw tag.
   Falls back to the tag when a route has no matching listing, which happens when a host we
   measured has since stopped being listed. */
const tagNames: Record<string, string> = {};
if (market.hook.model === m?.model) {
  for (const h of market.hook.hosts) {
    tagNames[h.tag] = h.provider + (h.variant ? ` ${h.variant}` : '');
  }
}
const routeLabel = (tag: string) => tagNames[tag] ?? tag;

const pairMax = pair ? Math.max(pair.cheap.cost_per_task, pair.dear.cost_per_task) : 1;
const pairBar = (v: number) => (v / pairMax) * PCW;

/* The cohort strip: every route the night measured, so the pair reads as two points inside a
   controlled comparison rather than as the whole experiment. The two under discussion carry
   the ink and the other nine recede. Linear rather than logarithmic — the whole range is
   3.4-fold, which a linear axis shows honestly and without a scale a reader has to decode.

   It shares the hero's grid rather than having one of its own: the same right-aligned label
   column ending at PL, and a data area starting at PC1 and ending where the hero's rules end,
   so the cheapest dot sits directly under the first host's column and the two panels read as
   one block. Three stacked rows — dots, then each marked host's cost, then its name — each
   lining up with one line of the label column. */
const STRIP_W = 800, STRIP_H = 100;
/* Full width now that the strip carries no label column of its own: the line above it names
   it, so repeating that inside the panel was the third time the section said the same thing. */
const SL = 90, SRX = 710;
const SY = 32, S_PRICE_Y = 60, S_NAME_Y = 82;
const routes = m ? [...m.routes].sort((a: any, b: any) => a.cost_per_task - b.cost_per_task) : [];
const rLo = routes.length ? routes[0].cost_per_task : 0;
const rHi = routes.length ? routes[routes.length - 1].cost_per_task : 1;
const rx = (v: number) =>
  SL + (rHi === rLo ? 0 : (v - rLo) / (rHi - rLo)) * (SRX - SL);
const isPairTag = (tag: string) => !!pair && (tag === pair.cheap.tag || tag === pair.dear.tag);
/* The two marked hosts, cheapest first, so the price row and the name row are generated from
   one list and cannot fall out of step with each other. Both labels anchor to the left of
   their own dot: centring them would push the cheapest host's label back over the label
   column, since its dot sits exactly on the column's left edge. */
const marked = pair ? [pair.cheap, pair.dear] : [];

/* 2. The ladder: cheapest to dearest listed host of each model, on a log axis.
   Prices run from two cents to six dollars, so a linear axis puts eleven of the
   thirteen rows inside the leftmost eighth of the width.

   Ordered by the mean price across every company serving the model, dearest first. That is
   what "this model is expensive" means, and it is not the midpoint of the two ends: Kimi K2.7
   Code is served at $0.68 to $0.86 by most of its companies and at $1.90 by one, so its bar
   looks high-centred while its average sits below GLM 5.2's.

   Which is why the mean is now drawn as a tick on each bar. Sorting rows by a number the
   chart does not show reads as a sorting fault, and the tick earns its place twice over: it
   also says whether a range is skewed, which two endpoints alone cannot. */
const ladder = market.models
  .filter((x: any) => x.open_weights && x.price_in && x.price_in.n > 1)
  .map((x: any) => ({ short: x.short, id: x.id, or_url: x.or_url, ...x.price_in }))
  .sort((a: any, b: any) => b.mean - a.mean);
/* LL moved out from 168 to make room for a brand mark and a left-aligned name. The names were
   right-aligned against the plot area, which cannot work with a mark beside them: the mark
   would have to sit at a different x on every row, and SVG has no text metrics at build time
   to place it. A fixed mark column with the name after it is what the model table does. */
const LW = 800, LROW = 30, LL = 196, LR = 66, LT = 26;
const L_ICON = 12;
const LH = LT + ladder.length * LROW + 40;
const lgLo = 0.01, lgHi = 10;
const lx = (v: number) =>
  LL + ((lg(v) - lg(lgLo)) / (lg(lgHi) - lg(lgLo))) * (LW - LL - LR);
const ly = (i: number) => LT + i * LROW + LROW / 2;
const lTicks = [0.01, 0.1, 1, 10];
/* The widest and narrowest spreads, found rather than read off the first and last row. Those
   two rows meant the widest and the narrowest while the ladder was sorted by spread; it is
   sorted by average price now, so taking row one would have quietly started naming whichever
   model happened to be dearest. */
const ladderMax = ladder.reduce((a: any, b: any) => (b.ratio > a.ratio ? b : a), ladder[0]);
const ladderMin = ladder.reduce((a: any, b: any) => (b.ratio < a.ratio ? b : a), ladder[0]);

/* 3. The capability matrix: one dot per live host, hollow where the host does not
   advertise tool calling. Hosts sit in listed-price order, which is the only
   ordering on this page that is not a judgement of a host. */
const caps = market.models
  .filter((x: any) => x.open_weights && (x.caps_grid ?? []).length > 1)
  .map((x: any) => ({
    short: x.short, or_url: x.or_url, grid: x.caps_grid,
    missing: x.caps_grid.filter((g: any) => !g.tools).length,
  }))
  .sort((a: any, b: any) => b.missing - a.missing || b.grid.length - a.grid.length);
const capCols = Math.max(...caps.map((c: any) => c.grid.length));
const CW = 800, CCELL = 19, CL = 176, CT = 32;
const CH = CT + caps.length * CCELL + 12;
const capsTotal = caps.reduce((n: number, c: any) => n + c.grid.length, 0);
const capsMissing = caps.reduce((n: number, c: any) => n + c.missing, 0);

/* 4. Precision disclosure across every live endpoint, as one bar. Grouped by
   width, because seven names leave three categories under five endpoints each. */
const prec = market.precision;
const PW = 800, PBAR = 52, PGAP = 2;
/* ---- the experiment card ----
   The measured pair was four comparable rows: price, completion, cost, reasoning. They are not
   equally important, and giving them one shape made the module read as a worksheet. Rebuilt as
   a hierarchy: the two controlled facts compact at the top, the cost gap taking most of the
   visual weight, and reasoning demoted to the sentence that explains it.

   HTML rather than SVG, unlike the charts here. The only graphical part is two bars, and a
   percentage width draws those in CSS. It buys the thing an 800-unit viewBox costs: the card
   reflows on a phone instead of needing a sideways swipe. */
const expDear = pair ? pair.dear.cost_per_task : 0;
const expCheap = pair ? pair.cheap.cost_per_task : 0;
const expPct = (v: number) => (expDear ? (v / expDear) * 100 : 0);
const expCheaperBy = pair
  ? Math.round((1 - pair.dear_listed / pair.cheap_listed) * 100) : 0;
const expThink = pair
  ? pair.dear.reasoning_per_task / pair.cheap.reasoning_per_task : 0;

/* ---- tool calling, as one bar per model ----
   The dot matrix was credible and cryptic: a reader had to learn that a dot is a company, that
   rows have different lengths, that filled means tools and a ring means none, and then count
   the exceptions themselves. The insight underneath is one sentence, so the chart should
   support a statement rather than hide one.

   Only models with a gap are drawn, worst first. Bar length is proportional to how many
   companies serve the model, so breadth still reads; the split inside it is supported against
   not. The models with no gap collapse into a single line, because seven full bars would say
   nothing seven times. */
const capBars = market.models
  .filter((x: any) => x.open_weights && (x.caps_grid ?? []).length > 1)
  .map((x: any) => {
    const grid = x.caps_grid;
    const miss = grid.filter((g: any) => !g.tools).length;
    return { short: x.short, or_url: x.or_url, total: grid.length, ok: grid.length - miss, miss };
  })
  .sort((a: any, b: any) => b.miss - a.miss || b.total - a.total);
const capGap = capBars.filter((c: any) => c.miss > 0);
const capClean = capBars.filter((c: any) => c.miss === 0);
const capMaxTotal = capGap.length ? Math.max(...capGap.map((c: any) => c.total)) : 1;
const capWorst = capGap[0];
const capPairs = capBars.reduce((n: number, c: any) => n + c.total, 0);
const capMissing = capBars.reduce((n: number, c: any) => n + c.miss, 0);

/* ---- precision, as flex ratios rather than SVG geometry, so the bar can be built the same way as
   the capability bars it now sits beside: one track, four segments, one row tall.

   Two states, matching the rest of the page: a stated width is filled and "not stated" is
   outlined. The three stated widths take three depths of one ink because they are ordered, and
   nothing here says a narrower number is worse — we have not measured that. */
const PREC_DEPTH: Record<string, string> = {
  '16-bit': 'mk-p-16', '8-bit': 'mk-p-8', '4-bit': 'mk-p-4', 'Other stated': 'mk-p-4',
  'Not stated': 'mk-p-none',
};
const precSegs = prec.buckets.map((b: any) => ({
  ...b, cls: PREC_DEPTH[b.label] ?? 'mk-p-4', pct: (b.n / prec.total) * 100,
}));
const precNone = prec.buckets.find((b: any) => b.label === 'Not stated');
const precFour = prec.buckets.find((b: any) => b.label === '4-bit');

/* 5. The scatter, on a log price axis for the same reason the ladder is, and now one per
   model with the model name as a selector rather than a title.

   NO CLIENT JAVASCRIPT, which is the constraint that shapes this. A dropdown that filters a
   chart is a script, and this page ships none. So every model's chart is rendered at build
   time and CSS shows the one whose radio is checked: ten hidden radios, a row of labels
   styled as chips, and generated rules pairing the two. The cost is markup for ten charts in
   one document; the gain is that a visitor changes model with no request and no script, and
   the page does not grow by ten charts' worth of height.

   Ten rather than fourteen: a model needs at least five companies reporting both a price and
   a first-token median before a scatter says anything, which drops Nemotron 3 Super,
   Ling-3.0-flash and Qwen3 Coder Next, and Claude Opus 5 goes for the same reason it is
   absent from the ladder — no public weights. */
const W = 800, H = 372, L = 62, R = 22, T = 32, B = 54;
const SCATTER_MIN_PTS = 5;
const SCATTER_DEFAULT = 'openai/gpt-oss-120b';
/* How many chips stay on the rail. The rest hide behind a "more" toggle, because ten model
   names in three rows is a wall and the point of the rail is to show at a glance that this is
   not a one-model dataset. */
const RAIL_VISIBLE = 5;

/* Ticks derived per model rather than from one fixed list. A shared list of decade values left
   GLM 5.1 with a single tick at $1, because its whole range is 97 cents to $1.54 and nothing
   else fell inside it. Decade steps first; if a range is narrow enough that fewer than three
   land in it, the finer sequence fills it in. */
const logTicks = (lo: number, hi: number) => {
  const build = (steps: number[]) => {
    const out: number[] = [];
    for (let k = -3; k <= 2; k++) {
      for (const st of steps) {
        const v = st * 10 ** k;
        if (v >= lo && v <= hi) out.push(v);
      }
    }
    return out;
  };
  const coarse = build([1, 2, 5]);
  return coarse.length >= 3 ? coarse : build([1, 1.5, 2, 3, 5, 7]);
};
/* Trims the trailing zeros a fixed precision would leave, so a tick reads $2 and $0.05 rather
   than $2.00 and $0.05 side by side. */
const tickLabel = (v: number) => `$${String(+v.toFixed(3))}`;

const scatterCards = market.models
  .filter((x: any) => x.open_weights)
  .map((x: any) => ({ x, pts: (x.scatter ?? []).filter((p: any) => p.price && p.ttft) }))
  .filter((c: any) => c.pts.length >= SCATTER_MIN_PTS)
  /* The default first, then most companies first. The default is gpt-oss-120b because its
     story needs no explaining: twenty companies serve it, the dearest charges nearly twelve
     times the cheapest, and six of them charge exactly the same price for first-token times
     that differ four-fold. */
  .sort((a: any, b: any) =>
    (b.x.id === SCATTER_DEFAULT ? 1 : 0) - (a.x.id === SCATTER_DEFAULT ? 1 : 0)
    || b.pts.length - a.pts.length)
  .map(({ x, pts }: any) => {
    /* Latency figures come from companies currently serving requests only. A paused company
       still reports a median, and it is whatever it was when it stopped. Its point is still
       drawn, hollow, because being listed and not serving is worth seeing — it is only kept
       out of the arithmetic. */
    const live = pts.filter((p: any) => p.status === 0);
    const tts = live.map((p: any) => p.ttft);
    const prices = pts.map((p: any) => p.price);
    const lo = Math.min(...prices) / 1.6, hi = Math.max(...prices) * 1.6;
    const yMax = Math.max(...pts.map((p: any) => p.ttft)) * 1.1;

    /* The largest group of companies charging one identical price, and the spread of
       first-token times inside it. This is the finding the chart exists for, and every one of
       the ten has one. */
    const buckets: Record<string, any[]> = {};
    live.forEach((p: any) => (buckets[p.price.toFixed(3)] ??= []).push(p));
    const tie = Object.entries(buckets)
      .map(([price, group]) => ({ price: Number(price), group }))
      .sort((a, b) => b.group.length - a.group.length)[0];

    return {
      id: x.id, short: x.short, or_url: x.or_url, listed: x.hosts_listed,
      priceRatio: x.price_in?.ratio,   /* the LISTED spread, so it matches the ladder's column */
      pts, live,
      ttftRatio: tts.length > 1 ? Math.max(...tts) / Math.min(...tts) : null,
      sx: (v: number) => L + ((lg(v) - lg(lo)) / (lg(hi) - lg(lo))) * (W - L - R),
      sy: (v: number) => T + (1 - v / yMax) * (H - T - B),
      xt: logTicks(lo, hi),
      yt: Array.from({ length: 4 }, (_, i) => (yMax / 3) * i),
      tie: tie && tie.group.length > 2 ? {
        price: tie.price, n: tie.group.length,
        lo: Math.min(...tie.group.map((p: any) => p.ttft)),
        hi: Math.max(...tie.group.map((p: any) => p.ttft)),
      } : null,
    };
  });

/* The pairing rules for the model rail and its ten charts. The cards are a direct sibling of the
   radios here, so no descendant step is needed. */
const scatterCss = selectorCss(scatterCards.length);

/* The model reference. This replaced a four-row "market pulse" table whose rows were four
   unrelated superlatives — a price ratio, a latency ratio, a cost ratio and a token count —
   stacked under one column headed "Spread", which invited comparing numbers that share no
   unit. It also buried the model in prose in the third column, and three of its four figures
   are now stated properly in the chart captions below.
   Rows are models, columns are one measure each, which is what a table is for. Sorted by host
   count rather than by spread: this is the block a reader uses to find their own model, and a
   sort key that reshuffles on every price move makes that harder. */
const reference = market.models
  .filter((x: any) => x.open_weights && x.price_in)
  .sort((a: any, b: any) => b.hosts_listed - a.hosts_listed || b.price_in.ratio - a.price_in.ratio);
---

<Layout
  title="CanaryOne Market — what the same open-weight model costs to run"
  description="Track how open-weight models differ across hosts in price, latency, capabilities and real task economics. Market intelligence measured by CanaryOne."
  path="/market"
>
  <div class="page-band">
    <header class="page-head">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="kicker">CANARYONE MARKET</p>
        <h1>The open-weight market, measured.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Open-weight models are portable. Their economics aren't. We track identical model
          weights across the companies serving them to see what actually changes: price,
          latency, capabilities, and the cost of completing real work.
        </p>
        {/* One line of standing, not a stat row. Four facts and no figure that needs a date
            beside it, because every dated figure sits next to its own chart below. */}
        <p class="mk-auth">
          {market.totals.models_published} models · {market.totals.host_endpoints} live
          endpoints · market read several times a day · measured workload sweeps
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>
  </div>

  <section class="tm">
    <div class="container">

      {pair && (
        <>
          <h2>
            Same model. Same tasks. Cheaper tokens. {mult(pair.ratio)} the cost.
          </h2>
          {/* The experiment's own header line. Says who measured it, on what, at what scale
              and when, before any figure appears. */}
          <p class="micro">
            Measured by CanaryOne · {m.model_name} · {m.routes.length} routes ·
            {' '}{m.tasks} coding tasks each · {day(m.night + 'T00:00:00Z')}
          </p>

          <div class="tm-panel">
            {/* What was held, or near enough. Two rows, no operator glyphs: the third column
                says in words what a "<" between two numbers was asking the reader to infer. */}
            <dl class="mk-ctrl">
              <div>
                <dt>Advertised token price</dt>
                <dd>{usdCol(pair.dear_listed)} vs {usdCol(pair.cheap_listed)}</dd>
                <dd class="mk-ctrl-note">
                  {routeLabel(pair.dear.tag)} {expCheaperBy} per cent cheaper
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Tasks completed</dt>
                <dd>{pair.dear.passed} of {pair.dear.attempted} vs
                  {' '}{pair.cheap.passed} of {pair.cheap.attempted}</dd>
                <dd class="mk-ctrl-note">Same outcome</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>

            {/* The reveal. The dearer bar carries the accent because the colour is saying "this
                is the finding", not "these are two series" — which is why only one of them has
                it. */}
            <p class="micro">Cost per successful task</p>
            <div class="mk-rev">
              <p class="mk-rev-name">{routeLabel(pair.dear.tag)}</p>
              <p class="mk-rev-val">{usd4(expDear)}</p>
              <div class="mk-rev-track">
                <div class="mk-rev-bar mk-rev-bar-hi" style={`width:${expPct(expDear)}%`}></div>
              </div>
              <p class="mk-rev-delta">{mult(pair.ratio)} higher</p>

              <p class="mk-rev-name">{routeLabel(pair.cheap.tag)}</p>
              <p class="mk-rev-val">{usd4(expCheap)}</p>
              <div class="mk-rev-track">
                <div class="mk-rev-bar" style={`width:${expPct(expCheap)}%`}></div>
              </div>
              <p class="mk-rev-delta"></p>
            </div>

            {/* COPY.md § Prose: a multiplier is spelled out in prose and the compact form is
                for a table cell, a chart label or a heading. This read "a 27× difference" and
                opened on a verbless fragment. */}
            <p class="mk-why">
              <strong>Why the gap?</strong> On the same job, one route spent
              {' '}{moreThan(expThink, 'many')} the other in reasoning tokens per task —
              {' '}{int(pair.dear.reasoning_per_task)}
              {' '}against {int(pair.cheap.reasoning_per_task)}.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p class="micro mk-strip-head">All {routes.length} routes measured</p>
          <div class="tm-panel mk-wrap">
            <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${STRIP_W} ${STRIP_H}`} class="mk-svg" role="img"
                 aria-label={`Cost per successful task across all ${routes.length} routes, from $${rLo.toFixed(4)} to $${rHi.toFixed(4)}. The two hosts discussed above sit at $${pair.cheap.cost_per_task.toFixed(4)} and $${pair.dear.cost_per_task.toFixed(4)}.`}>
              {/* The label column, right-aligned to the same edge as the comparison above, one
                  line per data row. The full range lives here rather than as a pair of axis
                  end labels, because whether an end of the axis belongs to a marked host
                  changes from night to night: on 9 August the dearest of the eleven was a
                  third company, and a figure at the right-hand end with a name under the row
                  below would have read as that company's number. */}
              <line x1={SL} x2={SRX} y1={SY} y2={SY} class="mk-grid" />
              {routes.map((r: any) => (
                <circle cx={rx(r.cost_per_task)} cy={SY} r={isPairTag(r.tag) ? R_LG : R_SM}
                        class={isPairTag(r.tag) ? 'mk-strip-dot mk-strip-dot-on' : 'mk-strip-dot'}>
                  <title>{`${routeLabel(r.tag)} · ${usd4(r.cost_per_task)} per successful task · ${r.passed} of ${r.attempted} passed`}</title>
                </circle>
              ))}
              {marked.map((r: any) => (
                /* Anchored to the end rather than the start once a dot is near the right edge,
                   because a marked host can also be the dearest of the cohort — on this night
                   it is — and a label starting at that dot runs off the panel. */
                <g class={rx(r.cost_per_task) > SRX - 90 ? 'mk-ax-end-r' : ''}>
                  <text x={rx(r.cost_per_task)} y={S_PRICE_Y} class="mk-ax mk-ax-row">
                    {usd4(r.cost_per_task)}
                  </text>
                  <text x={rx(r.cost_per_task)} y={S_NAME_Y} class="mk-ax mk-ax-row">
                    {routeLabel(r.tag)}
                  </text>
                </g>
              ))}
            </svg>
          </div>

          <div class="evidence">
            <p class="evidence-note">
            Measured workload, run on the night of
            {' '}{day(m.night + 'T00:00:00Z')}. Total spend divided by the tasks that
            completed, so failed work stays in the cost. These figures cost money to reproduce
            and hold that date until the next run. The size of a gap repeats from night to
            night; which host sits at either end does not, so read the multiple rather than the
            names.
          </p>
          </div>
          {/* The highest-intent moment on the page: the reader has just learned that advertised
              token economics can mislead. One action, and no second link competing with it. */}
          <p class="mk-ask">
            <strong>What does this look like on your workload?</strong>
            <a href="/evals">Benchmark your workload →</a>
          </p>
        </>
      )}



      <h2>The same weights are priced very differently.</h2>
      <p class="sec-note">
        Each bar runs from the cheapest company serving that model to the dearest, with a tick
        at the average of all of them. Dearest on average first. The axis is logarithmic: the
        models are three hundred times apart in price.
      </p>
      <p class="mk-scope">
        {ladder.length} models · {market.totals.host_endpoints} live endpoints
      </p>

      <div class="tm-panel mk-wrap">
        <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${LW} ${LH}`} class="mk-svg" role="img"
             aria-label={`Listed input price range across hosts for ${ladder.length} open-weight models. Every model's hosts differ in price, from ${ladderMin.ratio.toFixed(2)} times on the narrowest to ${ladderMax.ratio.toFixed(1)} times on the widest. The table lower down the page gives every figure.`}>
          <g>
            <circle cx={LL + 6} cy="12" r={R_SM} class="mk-end mk-end-lo" />
            <text x={LL + 18} y="16" class="mk-ax">Cheapest company</text>
            <line x1={LL + 156} x2={LL + 156} y1="5" y2="19" class="mk-mean" />
            <text x={LL + 166} y="16" class="mk-ax">Average of all of them</text>
            <circle cx={LL + 330} cy="12" r={R_SM} class="mk-end" />
            <text x={LL + 342} y="16" class="mk-ax">Dearest company</text>
          </g>
          {lTicks.map((t) => (
            <g>
              <line x1={lx(t)} x2={lx(t)} y1={LT} y2={LT + ladder.length * LROW} class="mk-grid" />
              <text x={lx(t)} y={LH - 22} class="mk-ax mk-ax-c">
                {t < 1 ? `$${t.toFixed(2)}` : `$${t.toFixed(0)}`}
              </text>
            </g>
          ))}
          {ladder.map((r: any, i: number) => (
            /* The band is the hover target and the highlight in one, and it is drawn first so
               it sits behind the marks. `fill: transparent` rather than `none`, because a
               `none` fill takes no pointer events and the row would only respond on the two
               dots. It spans the full width so the model name and the ratio highlight with
               their own row. */
            <g class="mk-row" data-model={r.id}>
              <rect x="0" y={ly(i) - LROW / 2} width={LW} height={LROW} class="mk-row-band" />
              {brandIconFor(r.id) && (
                <image href={brandIconFor(r.id)} x="6" y={ly(i) - L_ICON / 2}
                       width={L_ICON} height={L_ICON} />
              )}
              <text x={6 + L_ICON + 8} y={ly(i) + 4} class="mk-ax mk-ax-row">{r.short}</text>
              <line x1={lx(r.min)} x2={lx(r.max)} y1={ly(i)} y2={ly(i)} class="mk-range" />
              <line x1={lx(r.mean)} x2={lx(r.mean)} y1={ly(i) - 6} y2={ly(i) + 6} class="mk-mean">
                <title>{`${r.short} · ${usd(r.mean)} per 1M averaged across ${r.n} companies`}</title>
              </line>
              <circle cx={lx(r.min)} cy={ly(i)} r={R_SM} class="mk-end mk-end-lo">
                <title>{`${r.short} · cheapest listed host ${usd(r.min)} per 1M`}</title>
              </circle>
              <circle cx={lx(r.max)} cy={ly(i)} r={R_SM} class="mk-end">
                <title>{`${r.short} · dearest listed host ${usd(r.max)} per 1M`}</title>
              </circle>
              <text x={LW - 8} y={ly(i) + 4} class="mk-ax mk-ax-end-r">{mult(r.ratio)}</text>
            </g>
          ))}
          <text x={LL} y={LH - 4} class="mk-ax">
            Listed input price per 1M tokens · right column is dearest divided by cheapest
          </text>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <div class="evidence">
        <p class="evidence-note">
        There is no model here whose hosts agree on a price. At the narrowest, {ladderMin.short},
        the dearest company still charges {times(ladderMin.ratio)} the cheapest. At the widest,
        {' '}{ladderMax.short}, {ladderMax.n} companies serve the same weights and the dearest
        charges {times(ladderMax.ratio)} the cheapest. Metadata read {captured}.
        {' '}<a href={ladderMax.or_url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Check the widest one on
        OpenRouter →</a>
      </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The same price doesn't buy the same speed.</h2>
      <p class="sec-note">
        Every point is one company serving the same weights. Lower and further left is cheaper
        and faster, and the shaded band holds the companies charging an identical price.
      </p>

      {/* Ten radios, a row of labels and ten pre-rendered charts. The radios are the state,
          CSS does the switching, and no script is involved. .sr-only keeps them reachable by
          keyboard while the label carries the visible chip. */}
      <fieldset class="sel">
        <legend class="sr-only">Model</legend>
        {scatterCards.map((c: any) => (
          <input class="sr-only" type="radio" name="mk-model" id={`mk-m-${c.id}`}
                 checked={c.id === SCATTER_DEFAULT} />
        ))}
        {/* Last among the inputs on purpose: the generated rules index the radios with
            nth-of-type, so this must not sit among them. */}
        <input class="sr-only sel-all" type="checkbox" id="mk-all" />

        {/* The rail sits above the chart. Five names show, the rest arrive with the toggle, and
            a chip stays visible whenever its own model is the one selected. */}
        <div class="sel-rail">
          {scatterCards.map((c: any, i: number) => (
            <label for={`mk-m-${c.id}`} class={i < RAIL_VISIBLE ? '' : 'sel-more'}>
              {c.short}
            </label>
          ))}
          <label class="sel-toggle sel-show" for="mk-all">
            +{scatterCards.length - RAIL_VISIBLE} more
          </label>
          <label class="sel-toggle sel-hide" for="mk-all">Show fewer</label>
        </div>

        <div class="sel-cards">
          {scatterCards.map((c: any) => (
            <div>
              <p class="mk-sum">
                <strong>{c.listed} companies</strong> · {mult(c.priceRatio)} price spread
                {c.ttftRatio && <> · {mult(c.ttftRatio)} first-token spread</>}
              </p>
              <div class="tm-panel mk-wrap">
                <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H}`} class="mk-svg" role="img"
                     aria-label={`Listed price against reported first-token time for ${c.pts.length} companies serving ${c.short}. Price runs ${mult(c.priceRatio)} from cheapest to dearest.${c.tie ? ` ${c.tie.n} of them charge exactly ${priceWords(c.tie.price)} per million tokens, and their first token arrives after between ${(c.tie.lo / 1000).toFixed(1)} and ${(c.tie.hi / 1000).toFixed(1)} seconds.` : ''}`}>
                  {/* The equal-price cluster, drawn first so the dots sit on top of it. A
                      wash rather than a rule, because on cream yellow is a fill behind dark
                      content and never a line. It is the finding this chart exists for: the
                      companies inside the band charge the identical price. */}
                  {c.tie && (
                    <g>
                      <rect x={c.sx(c.tie.price) - 11} y={T} width="22" height={H - T - B}
                            rx="3" class="mk-band" />
                      <text x={c.sx(c.tie.price)} y={T - 8} class="mk-ax mk-ax-c mk-ax-row">
                        Same price: {c.tie.n} hosts at ${c.tie.price.toFixed(2)}
                      </text>
                    </g>
                  )}
                  {c.yt.map((t: number) => (
                    <g>
                      <line x1={L} x2={W - R} y1={c.sy(t)} y2={c.sy(t)} class="mk-grid" />
                      <text x={L - 10} y={c.sy(t) + 4} class="mk-ax mk-ax-y">
                        {(t / 1000).toFixed(1)}s
                      </text>
                    </g>
                  ))}
                  {c.xt.map((t: number) => (
                    <text x={c.sx(t)} y={H - 28} class="mk-ax mk-ax-c">{tickLabel(t)}</text>
                  ))}
                  <text x={8} y="14" class="mk-ax">First token, seconds</text>
                  <g>
                    <circle cx={L + 6} cy={H - 12} r={R_SM} class="mk-dot mk-dot-off" />
                    <text x={L + 18} y={H - 8} class="mk-ax">Not serving requests at this read</text>
                    <text x={W - R} y={H - 8} class="mk-ax mk-ax-end-r">
                      Listed price per 1M tokens, logarithmic
                    </text>
                  </g>
                  {c.pts.map((pt: any) => (
                    <circle cx={c.sx(pt.price)} cy={c.sy(pt.ttft)} r={R_SM}
                            class={pt.status === 0 ? 'mk-dot' : 'mk-dot mk-dot-off'}>
                      <title>{`${pt.tag} · ${usd(pt.price)} per 1M · ${(pt.ttft / 1000).toFixed(1)}s first token${pt.status === 0 ? '' : ' · not serving'}`}</title>
                    </circle>
                  ))}
                </svg>
              </div>
              <div class="evidence">
                <p class="evidence-note">
                {c.listed} companies serve {c.short}, and the dearest charges
                {' '}{times(c.priceRatio)} the cheapest.
                {c.tie && (
                  <> {cap(spell(c.tie.n))} of them charge exactly {priceWords(c.tie.price)} per
                  million tokens, and inside that group the first token arrives after
                  {' '}{(c.tie.lo / 1000).toFixed(1)} seconds at one end and
                  {' '}{(c.tie.hi / 1000).toFixed(1)} seconds at the other. Paying the same does
                  not buy the same.</>
                )} First-token figures cover the {c.live.length} companies serving requests at
                this read, each one their own reported median over the previous half hour.
                Metadata read {captured}.
                {' '}<a href={c.or_url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Check these prices on
                OpenRouter →</a>
              </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <h2>Same weights. Different capabilities.</h2>
      <p class="claim">
        {capWorst.miss} of {capWorst.total} {capWorst.short} hosts don't advertise tool calling.
      </p>
      <p class="sec-note">
        One bar per model, longest where most companies serve it. Filled advertises tool
        calling and outlined does not. An agent that needs to call a function does not degrade
        on a host without it; the loop stops.
      </p>

      <div class="tm-panel">
        <ul class="mk-cap">
          {capGap.map((c: any) => (
            <li>
              <span class="mk-cap-name">{c.short}</span>
              <span class="mk-cap-track" style={`width:${(c.total / capMaxTotal) * 100}%`}>
                <span class="mk-cap-ok" style={`flex:${c.ok}`}
                      title={`${c.ok} of ${c.total} advertise tool calling`}></span>
                <span class="mk-cap-no" style={`flex:${c.miss}`}
                      title={`${c.miss} of ${c.total} do not`}></span>
              </span>
              <span class="mk-cap-num">{c.ok} / {c.total}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="mk-cap-rest">
          {cap(spell(capClean.length))} other models: every tracked host advertises tool calling.
        </p>

        {/* Precision, in the same panel and at the same bar height, because it is the second
            way the same weights differ by who serves them and not a section of its own. */}
        <div class="mk-prec">
          <p class="micro">
            Stated precision, across all {prec.total} live endpoints
          </p>
          <div class="mk-prec-track">
            {precSegs.map((seg: any) => (
              <span class={`mk-prec-seg ${seg.cls}`} style={`flex:${seg.n}`}
                    title={`${seg.n} of ${prec.total} endpoints · ${seg.label}`}></span>
            ))}
          </div>
          <ul class="mk-prec-key">
            {precSegs.map((seg: any) => (
              <li>
                <span class={`mk-prec-chip ${seg.cls}`}></span>
                {seg.n} {seg.label.toLowerCase()}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="evidence">
        <p class="evidence-note">
        {capMissing} of the {capPairs} company-and-model pairs we track do not advertise tool
        calling, and {precNone.n} of {prec.total} endpoints do not say what precision they serve
        at. Both are provider-advertised, not a CanaryOne functional test, and whether four bits
        changes an answer is a question about output that nothing on this page measures.
        Metadata read {captured}.
        {' '}<a href={capWorst.or_url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Check the capabilities on
        OpenRouter →</a>
      </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The market moves underneath you.</h2>
      <p class="sec-note">
        One box per company, showing what it charged for
        {' '}{hook.name.split(': ').pop()} across {hkN} reads since
        {' '}{day(hook.stamps[0])}. Identical weights in all {hkHosts.length}. Every box
        shares one vertical scale, and a line that drops away is a discount.
      </p>

      <p class="micro tm-group-label">
        Changed price during the week
        <span class="tm-picker-count">{hkMoved.length} of {hkHosts.length} companies</span>
      </p>
      <ul class="tm-picker-grid mk-tiles">
        {hkMoved.map((h: any) => (
          <li class="mk-tile">
            <p class="mk-tile-top">
              <span class="mk-tile-name">
                {h.provider}{h.variant && <span class="mk-tile-var"> {h.variant}</span>}
              </span>
              <span class="mk-tile-price">{usd(h.last)}</span>
            </p>
            <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${TW} ${TH}`} class="mk-spark" role="img"
                 aria-label={`${hkLabel(h)} charged $${h.first.toFixed(2)} per million tokens at the start of the week and $${h.last.toFixed(2)} at the end.`}>
              <path d={hkPath(h.points)} class="mk-spark-line" />
              <circle cx={tx(hkN - 1)} cy={ty(h.last)} r={R_SM} class="mk-spark-end" />
            </svg>
            <p class="mk-tile-foot">was {usd(h.first)}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <p class="sec-note mk-strip-lede">
        The other {hkFlat.length} never moved. One dot per company, stacked where several
        charge the same price. Hover a dot for its precision and context window.
      </p>
      <div class="tm-panel mk-wrap">
        <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${PAIR_W} ${FS_H}`} class="mk-svg" role="img"
             aria-label={`Listed price of the ${hkFlat.length} companies that held the same price all week, from $${fsLo.toFixed(3)} to $${fsHi.toFixed(3)} per million tokens. They sit on ${fsStacks.length} distinct prices, and ${fsBiggest.hosts.length} of them charge exactly $${fsBiggest.price.toFixed(2)}.`}>
          <text x={PL} y={FS_AX - 14} class="mk-ax mk-ax-y mk-ax-row">Held the same price all week</text>
          <text x={PL} y={FS_AX + 8} class="mk-ax mk-ax-y">{usd(fsLo)} to {usd(fsHi)} per 1M</text>
          <text x={PL} y={FS_AX + 30} class="mk-ax mk-ax-y">
            {hkFlat.length} of {hkHosts.length} companies
          </text>

          <line x1={PC1} x2={PC2 + PCW} y1={FS_AX} y2={FS_AX} class="mk-grid" />

          {fsPlaced.map((p: any) => (
            <circle cx={p.x} cy={FS_AX - 9 - p.row * FS_GAP} r={FS_DOT} class="mk-flat-dot">
              <title>{hkTip(p.host)}</title>
            </circle>
          ))}

          {fsLabelled.map((g: any) => (
            <>
              <text x={fsx(g.price)} y={FS_AX + 18} class="mk-ax mk-ax-c mk-ax-row">
                {usd(g.price)}
              </text>
              <text x={fsx(g.price)} y={FS_AX + 34} class="mk-ax mk-ax-c">
                {g.hosts.length} companies
              </text>
            </>
          ))}
        </svg>
      </div>

      {/* The table twin for the strip above. A hover may enhance a chart and must not be the
          only way to reach a value: there is no hover on a touch screen, and these 23
          companies' names and prices appear nowhere else on the page. Collapsed because the
          finding is the shape of the cluster and this is the lookup behind it. */}
      <details class="tm-table">
        <summary>The {hkFlat.length} companies that held their price, as a table</summary>
        <div class="tm-table-scroll">
          <table class="tm-data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Company</th>
                <th>Price per 1M</th>
                <th>Precision</th>
                <th>Context</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {hkFlat.map((h: any) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{hkLabel(h)}</th>
                  <td>{usdCol(h.last)}</td>
                  <td>{h.quant && h.quant !== 'unknown' ? h.quant : 'not stated'}</td>
                  <td>{h.context ? `${Math.round(h.context / 1024)}K` : '—'}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>
      <div class="evidence">
        <p class="evidence-note">
        The steepest move was a discount that cut one company's price by
        {' '}{Math.round((1 - 1 / hkSwing) * 100)} per cent before expiring above where the week
        started. Every price here is what a buyer pays after whatever discount was running at
        that moment. Metadata read {captured}.
        {' '}<a href={hook.or_url} rel="noopener" target="_blank">Check these prices on
        OpenRouter →</a>
      </p>
      </div>

      <h2>How we measure it.</h2>
      <p class="sec-note">
        Two layers, kept apart. Host metadata is public: prices, capabilities, advertised
        throughput and availability, read several times a day. Measured workloads are ours:
        the same task suite run against multiple hosts serving identical weights, where total
        spend is divided by the tasks that actually completed, so failed work stays in the
        economics instead of disappearing from it.
      </p>
      <div class="evidence">
        <p class="evidence-note">
        We compare hosts within one model and never treat different weights as equivalent
        workloads. Timestamps sit beside measurements. Snapshots are not trends, and this
        record is days old rather than months. Relative positions between hosts change
        between runs, so we report the size of a spread rather than naming a best host.
        Prices, capabilities and precision are what a company publishes about itself, and a
        published capability is a claim rather than a test we have run. First-token figures
        are each company's own reported median over the half hour before we read it.
      </p>
      </div>


      <h2>What happens on your workload?</h2>
      <p class="sec-note">
        Market averages can tell you where to look. Your own workload tells you what to deploy.
      </p>
      <p class="mk-cta">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/evals">Benchmark your workload →</a>
        {/* Not "/#waitlist": the opener in Layout binds a[href="#waitlist"] and nothing
            handles the hash on load, so a root-relative link landed on the home page with
            no dialog. The dialog is in the layout, so it is on this page too. */}
        <a href="#waitlist">Get early access →</a>
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Only marks that global.css has no equivalent for. Everything structural above is an
     existing .tm-* primitive. */
  /* A chart keeps its readable size and its panel scrolls, rather than the labels shrinking
     with the viewBox. --chart-min is the floor; above it every chart is fluid. */
  .mk-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  .mk-svg { width: 100%; min-width: var(--chart-min); height: auto; display: block; }

  /* One opacity for every dot that is not the subject of its chart, so the greys match between
     the flat-price strip and the cohort strip instead of sitting at 0.55 and 0.32. Local rather
     than in :root because it is an alpha on one page's marks, not a palette value. */
  .mk-svg { --mk-dim: 0.5; }
  .mk-grid { stroke: var(--line); stroke-width: 1; }

  /* Axis and label ink. Sizes come from the type scale; only the SVG geometry —
     stroke widths and radii — is numeric, because the scale has no token for the
     thickness of a mark and a 1px hairline is not layout rhythm. */
  .mk-ax { font-size: var(--text-2xs); fill: var(--muted); font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .mk-ax-y { text-anchor: end; }
  .mk-ax-c { text-anchor: middle; }
  .mk-ax-end-r { text-anchor: end; fill: var(--text); }
  .mk-ax-row { fill: var(--text); }

  /* The hook, as one small chart per company. Layout comes from .tm-picker-grid, which is
     already an auto-filling card grid; these rules only cover the tile itself, which has no
     primitive. A tile is not .tm-chip — that is a flex row built around a checkbox. */
  .mk-tiles { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-8); padding: 0; }
  .mk-tile {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--panel);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }
  .mk-tile-top {
    display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-3); margin: 0;
  }
  .mk-tile-name {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--text);
    overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .mk-tile-price {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  .mk-tile-foot {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-2xs); color: var(--muted);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; margin: 0;
  }
  .mk-tile-var { color: var(--muted); }
  .mk-spark { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .mk-spark-line { fill: none; stroke: var(--text); stroke-width: 1.5; stroke-linejoin: round; }
  .mk-spark-end { fill: var(--text); stroke: var(--panel); stroke-width: 1.5; }

  /* The flat-price strip. Grey rather than the series ink because no dot here is the subject:
     the shape of the cluster is. The panel-coloured stroke is the gap that keeps a stack of
     nine reading as nine dots rather than one column, and the hover darkens the dot under the
     pointer so the tooltip that follows is clearly attached to it. */
  .mk-flat-dot {
    fill: var(--muted); fill-opacity: var(--mk-dim);
    stroke: var(--panel); stroke-width: 1.5;
    transition: fill var(--dur-base) var(--ease), fill-opacity var(--dur-base) var(--ease);
  }
  .mk-flat-dot:hover { fill: var(--text); fill-opacity: 1; }

  /* The measured pair. One ink on both bars: the length is the comparison, and a second
     colour would say the same thing twice. The value sits above its own bar at the lede
     size so the two figures are what the eye lands on first. */
  .mk-hero-val {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold); fill: var(--text);
  }
  .mk-hero-bar { fill: var(--text); fill-opacity: 0.82; }

  /* The cohort strip. The nine routes not under discussion stay legible and visibly not the
     subject, which is what stops the pair reading as the whole experiment. */
  .mk-strip-dot { fill: var(--muted); fill-opacity: var(--mk-dim); stroke: var(--panel); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .mk-strip-dot-on { fill: var(--text); fill-opacity: 1; }
  .mk-strip-lede { margin-top: var(--space-7); }

  /* Ladder. Hollow at the cheapest end, solid at the dearest, so the direction of the
     bar is readable without reading the axis. */
  .mk-range { stroke: var(--line-strong); stroke-width: 3; stroke-linecap: round; }
  /* The average across a model's companies, and the key the rows are sorted by. A tick rather
     than a third dot: it reads as a position on the bar instead of a fourth kind of endpoint,
     and it stays legible where it lands close to one of the two ends. */
  .mk-mean { stroke: var(--muted); stroke-width: 2; }
  .mk-end { fill: var(--text); stroke: var(--panel); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .mk-end-lo { fill: var(--panel); stroke: var(--text); stroke-width: 1.5; }

  /* One row at a time carries the emphasis, so thirteen equally weighted rows get a focal
     point without the chart turning into confetti. The yellow is a wash behind content that
     keeps its own ink, which is the only thing yellow may do on cream besides sit behind dark
     text — a yellow connector would be a coloured line on cream, which DESIGN.md rules out
     and which would also recolour the one mark that carries the row's meaning. The connector
     darkening to --muted is what actually does the emphasis; the wash only says which row.
     Nothing is hover-gated: every figure here is also a row of the model table below. */
  .mk-row-band { fill: transparent; }
  .mk-row:hover .mk-row-band { fill: var(--accent-bg); }
  .mk-row:hover .mk-range { stroke: var(--muted); }

  .mk-dot { fill: var(--text); fill-opacity: 0.8; stroke: var(--panel); stroke-width: 1.5; }
  .mk-dot-off { fill: var(--panel); stroke: var(--muted); stroke-width: 1.5; }

  /* Same shape as .tm-chip, minus the checkbox row it is built around. Yellow arrives on the
     checked one through a generated rule, which is a fill behind dark text on cream and so one
     of the uses DESIGN.md allows. */

  /* Every card is rendered and all but one hidden. The generated rules turn one back on. */
  .mk-sum {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
  }
  .mk-sum strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: var(--weight-semibold); }

  /* The hero's authority line: four facts in one mono row on the dark band. */
  .mk-auth {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--muted-on-dark); margin: var(--space-5) 0 0;
  }
  /* The mid-page ask. A link, not a button: the button is the close, and two yellow buttons on
     one page would make neither of them the call to action. */
  /* Scope note trailing the breadth chart's own sentence, so the count sits with the claim
     rather than in a stat row of its own. */
  .mk-scope {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--muted);
    margin: calc(-1 * var(--space-3)) 0 var(--space-5);
  }
  /* Two controlled facts, three columns: what was held, the two values, and what that means in
     words. The third column is what the old "<" and "=" glyphs were asking a reader to infer. */
  .mk-ctrl { margin: 0 0 var(--space-8); display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); }
  .mk-ctrl > div {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--col-label)) minmax(0, var(--col-value)) 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-4); align-items: baseline;
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .mk-ctrl dt, .mk-ctrl dd { margin: 0; font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  .mk-ctrl dt { color: var(--muted); }
  .mk-ctrl dd { font-family: var(--font-mono); color: var(--text); }
  .mk-ctrl-note { color: var(--muted) !important; font-family: var(--font-sans) !important; }

  .mk-rev {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--col-label)) auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    gap: var(--space-3) var(--space-4); align-items: center; margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }
  .mk-rev p { margin: 0; }
  .mk-rev-name { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text); }
  .mk-rev-val {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-xl);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold); color: var(--text);
  }
  .mk-rev-track { background: var(--bg-tint); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); }
  .mk-rev-bar { height: var(--space-6); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); background: var(--text); }
  /* Canary on the dearer bar only. Yellow here is saying "this is the finding" rather than
     naming a series, which is why exactly one bar has it; --accent-deep rather than --accent
     because the lighter one has too little to say against a white panel. */
  .mk-rev-bar-hi { background: var(--accent-deep); }
  .mk-rev-delta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold); color: var(--text); white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* The mechanism, demoted. It explains the finding and is not another finding. */
  .mk-why { font-size: var(--text-base); color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }
  .mk-why strong { color: var(--text); }

  /* .micro, with the space above it that separates the cohort strip from the card it belongs
     to. Only the margin differs. */
  .mk-strip-head { margin-top: var(--space-8); }

  .mk-cap { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .mk-cap li {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--col-label)) minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    gap: var(--space-4); align-items: center;
  }
  .mk-cap-name { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text); }
  /* The track's own width carries how many companies serve the model, so breadth still reads
     off the chart; the split inside it is supported against not. */
  /* No border-radius and no overflow here, deliberately. Rounding the track and clipping the
     children curved the OUTER edge of an outlined segment's border while its inner edge stayed
     square, so the stroke wedged out at each corner and the end looked chipped. Each segment
     rounds its own outer corners instead — see the shared rule below — so a border follows a
     real curve on both sides. */
  .mk-cap-track { display: flex; height: var(--space-5); }
  /* Two states and no third colour. --danger was in the palette but read as a different design
     system: one saturated red block on a page whose ink is near-black and whose only accent is
     canary. Filled means the capability is advertised and outlined means it is not, which is the
     idiom the rest of the page already uses for absence — the hollow dot on the scatter for a
     company not serving, and the hollow end of a ladder bar for the cheapest. */
  .mk-cap-ok { background: var(--text); }
  .mk-cap-no {
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--text);
    border-left: 0;
  }
  .mk-cap-num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--muted);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  /* Precision, at the same bar height as the capability rows above it so the two read as one
     panel rather than two charts that happen to be adjacent. */
  .mk-prec { margin-top: var(--space-6); padding-top: var(--space-4); border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .mk-prec-track { display: flex; height: var(--space-5); }
  .mk-prec-seg { background: var(--text); }
  .mk-prec-key {
    list-style: none; margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; padding: 0;
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-5);
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--muted);
  }
  .mk-prec-key li { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); }
  .mk-prec-chip {
    width: var(--space-3); height: var(--space-3); border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background: var(--text); flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  /* AFTER both base rules above, and that ordering is the whole reason this works. The depth
     and outline classes sit on the segment and on the key chip, so at equal specificity they
     have to come last — defined earlier, .mk-prec-chip's own background won and "not stated"
     rendered as a filled square in the key while the bar itself was correctly outlined. */
  .mk-p-16 { opacity: 0.3; }
  .mk-p-8 { opacity: 0.55; }
  .mk-p-4 { opacity: 0.8; }
  /* The two segmented bars round their own ends. Individual corner properties rather than the
     shorthand, so the first-child and last-child rules set different corners and neither has to
     override the other — and a single segment that is both gets all four. */
  .mk-cap-track > *,
  .mk-prec-track > * { border-radius: 0; }
  .mk-cap-track > :first-child,
  .mk-prec-track > :first-child {
    border-top-left-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border-bottom-left-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }
  .mk-cap-track > :last-child,
  .mk-prec-track > :last-child {
    border-top-right-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border-bottom-right-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }

  /* Outlined, the same way a missing capability is. In the bar the left border drops out so the
     segment reads as continuous with the filled ones beside it; the key chip keeps all four.

     Compound selectors rather than a bare .mk-p-none, so this wins on specificity instead of on
     source order. Order alone was not enough: the built stylesheet had it last and correct, and
     the dev server emitted a different order, so the key chip rendered filled while the bar was
     right. Anything that must override a base rule here is a compound now. */
  .mk-prec-seg.mk-p-none,
  .mk-prec-chip.mk-p-none {
    background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--text); opacity: 1;
  }
  .mk-prec-seg.mk-p-none { border-left: 0; }

  .mk-cap-rest {
    font-size: var(--text-base); color: var(--muted);
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0; padding-top: var(--space-4); border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  /* CTA one: a claim and a single action, inside the section that earns it. */
  .mk-ask {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-md); margin: var(--space-7) 0 0;
  }
  .mk-band { fill: var(--accent-bg); }

  .mk-big { font-weight: 650; }
  .mk-cta {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-6);
    margin-top: var(--space-10);
  }
  /* LAST IN THE BLOCK ON PURPOSE. These override base rules of the same specificity, so source
     order decides and an earlier @media block simply loses — which is exactly what happened:
     the bars collapsed to zero width on a phone because the four-column rule still won.

     The three grids have fixed first columns, right on a wide column and wrong on a narrow
     one. Below the phone breakpoint they stack, and the bar takes the full width beneath. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .mk-ctrl > div,
    .mk-rev,
    .mk-cap li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-1); }
    .mk-rev { gap: var(--space-2); margin-bottom: var(--space-8); }
    .mk-rev-track { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
    .mk-cap-track { width: 100% !important; }
    .mk-cap li { padding-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  }
</style>

